Q: Setting largeSubtitleTextAttributes doesn&#39;t do anything A: <p>For iOS 26, you could consider using toolbar items instead. These allow much more styling to be applied than regular navigation titles.</p>
<ul>
<li>By applying the modifier <code>.toolbarTitleDisplayMode(.automatic)</code>, toolbar items with placement <code>.largeTitle</code> and <code>.largeSubtitle</code> automatically disappear when the content has been scrolled up and into the safe area region.</li>
<li>Likewise, a toolbar item with placement <code>.subtitle</code> automatically appears when the content has been scrolled up.</li>
<li>It is important that <code>.toolbarVisibility(.visible, for: .automatic)</code> is applied too, otherwise the toolbar items might not show at all.</li>
<li>The placements <code>.largeTitle</code>, <code>.largeSubtitle</code> and <code>.subtitle</code> are all new in iOS 26.</li>
<li>You might expect (or hope) that a toolbar item with placement <code>.title</code> should work like <code>.subtitle</code> and appear automatically when the content has been scrolled up. Unfortunately, this is not the case. In fact, when an item with placement <code>.title</code> is included, it messes up the automatic appearance of <code>.subtitle</code>. The reason may be because <code>.title</code> placement has existed since iOS 14, so Apple may be trying to provide some kind of backwards compatibility.</li>
<li>As a workaround for <code>.title</code> not fitting into the new way of things, the item with placement <code>.subtitle</code> can contain a <code>VStack</code> that shows both the title and the subtitle.</li>
<li>By default, the items with placement <code>.largeTitle</code> and <code>.largeSubtitle</code> are shown centered. If you want them to have leading alignment, a <code>.frame</code> modifier can be applied.</li>
<li>The modifiers <code>.navigationTitle</code> and <code>.navigationSubtitle</code> can be applied if they are still needed, but the toolbar items <code>.largeTitle</code> and <code>.largeSubtitle</code> take precedence for display. This is actually documented on the pages for these items (see <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/toolbaritemplacement/largetitle#discussion" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>largeTitle</code></a> and <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui/toolbaritemplacement/largesubtitle#discussion" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>largeSubtitle</code></a>).</li>
</ul>
<p>Here is a little example to show it all working:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct ContentView: View {
    let loremIpsum = &quot;Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat nulla pariatur. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum.&quot;

    var body: some View {
        NavigationStack {
            ScrollView {
                VStack(spacing: 40) {
                    ForEach(0..&lt;10) { _ in
                        Text(loremIpsum)
                    }
                }
                .padding()
            }
//            .navigationTitle(&quot;Title&quot;)
//            .navigationSubtitle(&quot;Subtitle&quot;)
            .toolbarTitleDisplayMode(.automatic)
            .toolbarVisibility(.visible, for: .automatic)
            .toolbar {
                ToolbarItem(placement: .largeTitle) {
                    Text(&quot;Large Title&quot;)
                        .font(.largeTitle)
                        .fontWeight(.semibold)
                        .foregroundStyle(.green)
                        .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)
                }
                ToolbarItem(placement: .largeSubtitle) {
                    Text(&quot;Large Subtitle&quot;)
                        .font(.subheadline)
                        .foregroundStyle(.orange)
                        .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, alignment: .leading)
                }
                ToolbarItem(placement: .subtitle) {
                    VStack {
                        Text(&quot;Title&quot;)
                            .font(.title3)
                            .fontWeight(.semibold)
                            .foregroundStyle(.blue)
                        Text(&quot;Subtitle&quot;)
                            .font(.footnote)
                            .foregroundStyle(.red)
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
